  "step_2": "The individual describes uncovering the options from top-left to bottom-left, then top-right to bottom-right, suggesting a visual or sequential inspection of the lotteries. They then focus on Lottery A’s first outcome (E1) and its probability, noting that the 80% chance of a loss is quite high. The individual expresses a preference for dealing with a certain loss rather than facing a high probability of a larger loss. Their justification centers on aversion to a high-probability loss and a desire for certainty in the magnitude of the loss. They do not mention any reference point derived from averaging outcomes, nor do they compare outcomes to such a reference. There is no indication of computing differences from an aspiration level or evaluating which lottery offers a better chance of exceeding such a level. The deliberation focuses on probability levels of loss and certainty, not on outcome comparisons relative to a computed mean.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on computing an aspiration level as the mean of all outcomes and then comparing the probabilities of outcomes exceeding this level. The individual does not mention or imply any calculation of an average outcome or use of such a value as a reference. Instead, their reasoning is based on the high probability of loss in Lottery A and a preference for the certainty of Lottery B. The information considered by the individual (80% loss probability, desire for fixed loss) is unrelated to the outcomes’ deviations from an aspiration level. Furthermore, the justification (certainty over high-probability loss) does not align with the reason’s rule (maximizing chance of positive deviation from aspiration). There is no evidence that the individual used the aspiration-level-based rule. The reasoning is more consistent with risk aversion or certainty effect, not aspiration-level reasoning.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
